    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the proximal humerus. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["High-dose radiation therapy", "Systemic chemotherapy", "Denosumab therapy", "Wide surgical resection", "Radiofrequency ablation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the posterior spine that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery", "Curettage and bone grafting", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Wide surgical resection", "Intravenous bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 21-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal tibia. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Surgical resection alone", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal pelvis. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old patient presents with back pain and fatigue. Radiographs of the skull and spine show multiple ''punched-out'' radiolucencies without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. Laboratory tests reveal hypercalcemia, anemia, and an M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. Which bone scan finding is expected?', '["Intensely increased uptake (''hot'' spots) in all lesions", "Increased uptake exclusively in the appendicular skeleton", "A ''superscan'' with absent renal visualization", "A falsely negative (''cold'' or normal) bone scan", "Diffuse periosteal uptake"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Multiple myeloma lesions are purely osteolytic, caused by myeloma cells stimulating osteoclasts (via RANKL) and actively inhibiting osteoblasts (via DKK-1). Because a Technetium-99m bone scan relies on osteoblastic activity (bone formation), it is notoriously unreliable (falsely negative) in multiple myeloma. A skeletal survey (plain films) or low-dose whole-body CT is the preferred imaging modality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal radius. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Denosumab", "Radiation therapy", "Bisphosphonates", "Amputation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 15-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the distal femur. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Surgical resection alone", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the pelvis. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Necrotic tumor debris"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 18-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the posterior spine that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Radiation therapy", "Curettage and bone grafting", "Wide surgical resection", "Intravenous bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.');
